              "explanation": "Unsigned eight-bit addition computes 250+10=260 as an unrestricted integer. Since an eight-bit output can store residues from zero through 255, divide 260 by 256: quotient one and remainder four. The low eight bits therefore contain 00000100, and unsigned carry-out is one. Check this by converting 250 to 11111010 and ten to 00001010 and performing column addition. The higher carry is not stored in the low-byte result. Signed arithmetic interprets the same bit pattern 11111010 as \u22126; \u22126+10=4 is representable, so the signed-overflow flag should be zero even though unsigned carry-out is one. This contrast catches the common error of using one flag as a universal indication of arithmetic overflow. If the program requires exact mathematical 260, it must use a wider result, detect the carry or apply appropriate saturation. If the ISA provides a narrowing conversion, its specified semantics determine whether the high bits are silently discarded. The answer is complete only when it states both the low output and the carry convention."
